      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on a low-fat mayo formulation and having issues with phase separation in high-acid environments. Can you explain the specific molecular mechanics of how lecithin acts as an emulsifier in this context, especially regarding the oil-water interface?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! In the realm of low-fat mayo, lecithin is the trusty bridge between the oil and water, much like a skilled diplomat between two feuding kingdoms. When you introduce lecithin into your concoction, it waltzes gracefully across the oil-water interface, its hydrophilic head reaching out to the water and its hydrophobic tail cozying up to the oil. This dual affinity allows it to act as a stabilizing agent, preventing the two phases from separating, even in the face of high-acid environments that would typically cause such a rift. Think of lecithin as the peacekeeper in a land of oil and water, ensuring harmony and preventing the chaos of separation.",
